{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And Moses brought out.</b> The sense: he brought them out of their tents to meet Elohim, because they accepted that HaShem, who descends upon Mount Sinai, would be for them as Elohim." "<b>And they stationed themselves at the bottom of the mountain.</b> Outside the boundary that Moses delimited for them. They were stationed at Mount Sinai in the same order in which Moses stationed their children in the fortieth year when he made a covenant with them. For at the head were the firstborn, who draw near to YHWH; after them, the heads of the tribes, who are the princes; after them, the elders; after them, the officers; after them, every man of Israel; after them, the children; after them, the women; after them, the converts. Moses and Aaron crossed the boundary and were on the mountain, close to the priests — even though Scripture does not state this explicitly, for Scripture held to a concise path, since we know that they did not rebel against the word of HaShem, for it is written, “Go down, and you shall come up, you and Aaron with you” (below, verse 24), and Moses said, “I stood between YHWH and you” (Deuteronomy 5:5)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:58.199615703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}